**1. PROVED — the trace identity holds at \(j_0=1\).** Write \(S_d\) for the sum of the roots of \(H_{-7d^2}\), and \(\chi(\ell)=\left(\frac{-7}{\ell}\right)\). For squarefree \(N\) coprime to \(14\),
\[
P_N(1)\equiv\sum_{d\mid N}S_d\prod_{\ell\mid N/d}(1+\chi(\ell))\equiv S_N\pmod2.
\]

Indeed, lift to the maximal-order CM curve with \(j=-3375\). Reduction preserves its odd-order kernels and their quotients. Classify a cyclic \(N\)-isogeny by the product \(d\) of primes at which it descends. Coprime kernels commute, so take the horizontal steps first. Each has \(1+\chi(\ell)\) choices, all returning to the unique maximal-order class. The subsequent descents reach every conductor-\(d\) class exactly once: at each prime the children are the fiber of the extension-of-ideals map on class groups, with a unique ascending parent. This proves the first equality. Every proper-divisor multiplier is even. [Sutherland, *Isogeny volcanoes*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5370)

**REFUTED — the absolute-trace generalization to arbitrary ordinary \(j_0\).** Already \(P_{15}(X)=X^7+X^{15}\) by the given theta series. For \(u\in\mathbf F_8\setminus\mathbf F_2\), this gives \(1+u\notin\mathbf F_2\). The general CM rule is to sum the descending fiber above **each** horizontal target, with multiplicity. Split horizontal ideals can act differently on the starting class. At primes dividing its conductor one must also include the ascending edge. Iterating these prime correspondences gives the general squarefree formula. [Sutherland](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5370)

**2. OPEN — fast factor-free evaluation.** I know no classical \(N^{o(1)}\) algorithm for the single ordinary value, even at \(1\), or a lower bound excluding one. The specific obstacles are these.

- **Class groups and Frobenius.** For \(N=pq\), the quotient group in the question has \(h=(p-\chi(p))(q-\chi(q))=\Theta(N)\) elements. Group arithmetic supplies labels. Computing the coordinate \(j(x)\) and summing it are additional operations. If \([\pi]\) has order \(r\), equivariance rewrites the sum as \(h/r\) field traces from degree \(r\). Explicit orbit enumeration still accounts for \(h\) coordinates. [CM class action](https://arxiv.org/abs/1208.5370)
- **Canonical lifts and local coordinates.** Serre–Tate coordinates deform a specified special fiber. A lifted prime-to-two isogeny relates the source and target deformation spaces once its reduction is specified. The missing global operation is to identify or aggregate the distinct target \(j\)-residues. Local continuation alone supplies no such sum. [Katz, *Serre–Tate local moduli*](https://web.math.princeton.edu/~nmk/old/serretatelocmod.pdf)
- **Trace formulas.** Gross–Zagier computes norms of differences. Zagier’s weight-\(3/2\) coefficient includes imprimitive forms, hence gives \(\sum_{d\mid N}S_d\bmod2\). Ordinary Möbius inversion uses the divisors of \(N\). Direct series expansion reaches index \(7N^2\), whereas the original reciprocal \(1/(1-j_0\Delta)\) needs precision only through \(N\). [Gross–Zagier](https://wstein.org/papers/bib/gross-zagier-on_singular_moduli.pdf) · [Zagier](https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/zagier/files/tex/TracesSingModuli/fulltext.pdf)
- **Duke’s series.** Multiplication by \(N\) bijects the proposed root sets when \((c,N)=1\) and can miss roots otherwise. Even granting the exponential sums, ordinary exact rounding requires absolute error below \(1/2\) on a trace of \(\Theta(N)\) bits. This bounds the explicit integer-reconstruction route only. [Duke](https://math.ucla.edu/~wdduke/preprints/modud.pdf)

**3. PROVED — quadratic growth in the Chebotarev limit.** Let \(G\) be Bellaïche’s image in \(\mathrm{SL}_2(A)\), \(A=\mathbf F_2[[x,y]]\), and \(K_s=\ker(G\to\mathrm{SL}_2(A/\mathfrak m^s))\). Then
\[
\log_2|G/K_{H+1}|=\Theta(H^2).
\]
The upper bound follows by counting matrix entries. For the lower bound take Frobenius matrices \(u,v\) at \(3,5\), of traces \(x,y\). They generate topologically by the Frattini criterion modulo \(8\). Thus \(B=vuv^{-1}-u\) is nonscalar: otherwise \(v\) permutes the two eigenlines of \(u\), and its nonzero trace excludes swapping them, contradicting absolute irreducibility. [Bellaïche, Theorems 1–2](https://people.brandeis.edu/~jbellaic/preprint/repgaluniv.pdf)

For \(s=2^a-1\), Cayley–Hamilton gives \(u^{2^a}=\lambda I+x^su\). Hence \([v,u^{2^a}]-I=x^sBu^{-2^a}\) has an off-diagonal entry of order \(s+O(1)\), since \(B\) is traceless nonscalar and \(u^{2^a}\to I\). Modulo \(\mathfrak m^{2s}\), deviations from identity in \(K_s\) add and are traceless. On their off-diagonal pairs, conjugation by \(w\) acts by its entrywise square. Adding the actions of \(w,w^{-1}\) therefore multiplies by \((\operatorname{tr}w)^2\). The projected image contains all \(x^{2i}y^{2j}\) multiples of the displayed pair. Those with \(2(i+j)<s-O(1)\) are independent by lowest homogeneous degree. This gives \(\Omega(s^2)\) bits, and consecutive such depths differ by a bounded factor.

**PROVED — the trace product retains quadratic Shannon entropy** for independent Haar Frobenii. Four fixed image matrices \(b_i\) span \(M_2(\operatorname{Frac}A)\) by absolute irreducibility. Thus the four traces \(\operatorname{tr}(b_i g)\) determine \(g\) up to constant precision loss by clearing denominators. Each has the Haar trace distribution, so entropy subadditivity gives \(\Omega(H^2)\) for one trace. With fixed positive probability the second trace has order one. Conditional on that trace, multiplication loses only one degree of precision. Nicolas–Serre duality identifies the resulting product \(T_pT_q\bmod\mathfrak m^{H+1}\) with \(v_H\). [Nicolas–Serre](https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.1039)

Conditioning on \(N\bmod 2^b\) costs at most \(b\) bits. Conditioning on **exact \(N\)** gives zero entropy. Chebotarev justifies the Haar calculation for fixed \(H\) as independently sampled primes in a balanced dyadic interval grow. It supplies no uniform conclusion at \(H=\mathrm{poly}(\log N)\). Fairness of \(P_N(1)\) remains **CONJECTURED**.

**OPEN — efficient decoding.** Even granting \(v_H\) for polynomial \(H\), no known reduction converts it into, for example, the leading \(\tfrac14\log_2 N+O(1)\) bits of one balanced factor, which would suffice. Bach–Charles instead uses exact characteristic-zero eigenvalues at \(N,N^2\). Producing such a decoder, or proving computational hardness for a specified inversion problem, remains open. [Coppersmith](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/pdf/Coppersmith1996a.pdf) · [Bach–Charles](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192)